.TH COPYSIGN 3 "2026-05-03" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
copysign, copysignf, copysignl \- copy sign of a floating-point number
.SH LIBRARY
Math library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "double copysign(double " x ", double " y );
.br
.BI "float copysignf(float " x ", float " y );
.br
.BI "long double copysignl(long double " x ", long double " y );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR copysign ()
family of functions returns a value whose magnitude is taken from the
first argument
.I x
and whose sign (sign bit) is taken from the second argument
.IR y .
.PP
The functions are defined for every value of
.I x
and
.IR y :
no input is rejected, no domain restriction applies, and no
floating-point exception is raised.  In IEC 60559 (IEEE\ 754) terms
.BR copysign ()
is a
.I non-arithmetic
operation: it rearranges the sign bit of an existing representation
rather than performing a numeric computation.
.PP
The sign of zero is significant.
.B +0.0
and
.B \-0.0
are distinct floating-point values that compare equal under the usual
relational operators but have different sign bits.  Because
.BR copysign ()
inspects the sign bit (not the algebraic sign), it is the canonical
portable way to fabricate a negative zero from a positive zero, or to
distinguish the two when used together with
.BR signbit (3).
.PP
For a NaN argument, the magnitude returned is a NaN of the same format
as
.IR x ;
the sign bit applied is taken from
.IR y .
The Substrate libm follows the common implementation choice of
returning a NaN whose payload is preserved from
.I x
and whose sign bit is that of
.IR y ,
so
.BI copysign(NaN, " +y" )
yields
.B +NaN
and
.BI copysign(NaN, " \-y" )
yields
.BR \-NaN .
The C standard leaves the sign of a NaN unspecified for arithmetic
operations, but
.BR copysign ()
is defined by both C99 Annex F and IEC 60559 to copy the sign bit of
.I y
unconditionally, including when either argument is NaN.
.PP
The
.BR copysignf ()
variant operates on
.B float
values; the
.BR copysignl ()
variant operates on
.B "long double"
values.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The
.BR copysign ()
family returns a value with the magnitude (absolute value) of
.I x
and the sign bit of
.IR y .
.PP
In particular:
.TP
.IR x " is finite, " y " is non-negative"
The result is
.BR fabs( x ) .
.TP
.IR x " is finite, " y " is negative (including \-0.0)"
The result is
.BR \-fabs( x ) .
.TP
.IR x " is \(+-infinity"
The result is
.B +infinity
if
.I y
is non-negative,
.B \-infinity
otherwise.
.TP
.IR x " is NaN"
The result is a NaN with the sign bit of
.IR y .
.PP
No errors are defined for these functions.  They never set
.I errno
and never raise a floating-point exception, even when an argument is
NaN, infinity, or signed zero.
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
.TS
allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Interface	Attribute	Value
T{
.BR copysign (),
.BR copysignf (),
.BR copysignl ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
.TE
.SH STANDARDS
.BR copysign (),
.BR copysignf (),
and
.BR copysignl ()
conform to C99 and POSIX.1-2008.  The semantics, including the
treatment of signed zero, infinity, and NaN, follow C99 Annex F.10.8.1
and the IEC 60559:1989 (IEEE\ 754) "copySign" operation.
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program shows two common uses of
.BR copysign ():
clamping the magnitude of one value to the sign of another (a sign
transfer that does not branch on
.IR y ),
and fabricating a signed zero from an unsigned literal:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    double mag  = 3.5;
    double sign = \-2.0;
    double signed_mag, neg_zero, pos_zero;

    /* Magnitude of |mag|, sign of sign. */
    signed_mag = copysign(mag, sign);
    printf("copysign(%.1f, %.1f) = %.1f\en", mag, sign, signed_mag);

    /* Fabricate \-0.0 from a positive literal. */
    neg_zero = copysign(0.0, \-1.0);
    pos_zero = copysign(0.0,  1.0);
    printf("copysign(0.0, \-1.0) = %g (signbit = %d)\en",
           neg_zero, signbit(neg_zero));
    printf("copysign(0.0,  1.0) = %g (signbit = %d)\en",
           pos_zero, signbit(pos_zero));

    /* Sign bit of \-0.0 is meaningful. */
    printf("copysign(1.0, \-0.0) = %.1f\en", copysign(1.0, \-0.0));
    printf("copysign(1.0, +0.0) = %.1f\en", copysign(1.0,  0.0));

    /* Infinity and NaN propagate the magnitude. */
    printf("copysign(INF, \-1) = %g\en", copysign(INFINITY, \-1.0));
    printf("copysign(NAN, \-1) signbit = %d\en",
           signbit(copysign(NAN, \-1.0)));

    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
A representative output is:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
copysign(3.5, \-2.0) = \-3.5
copysign(0.0, \-1.0) = \-0 (signbit = 1)
copysign(0.0,  1.0) = 0 (signbit = 0)
copysign(1.0, \-0.0) = \-1.0
copysign(1.0, +0.0) = 1.0
copysign(INF, \-1) = \-inf
copysign(NAN, \-1) signbit = 1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Note that
.BI copysign(1.0, " \-0.0" )
returns
.B \-1.0
even though
.B \-0.0
compares equal to
.BR +0.0 :
.BR copysign ()
inspects the sign bit, not the numeric value.
